- If you **don’t define any constructor**, Java provides a **default no-argument constructor** automatically.
- This default constructor:
  - Has no parameters.
  - Calls `super()` (i.e., the no-arg constructor of the superclass).
  - Initializes fields to default values (`0`, `null`, `false`, etc.).

```java
